--TEST--
`Core\Http\Client` carries at most one body, and two keys written at one call are refused while compiling — `rule:http-server/an-outbound-request-carries-one-body`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:http-server/an-outbound-request-carries-one-body`: which key a body is written under is what says
// how it is sent, so two of them is not a request with two bodies but a call
// site that has not decided what it is sending. Both halves of the question
// are written out — `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2 makes the bag a literal — so it is refused
// here rather than by whatever the server made of the first key it read.
string $url = "https://example.test/orders";

// The accepted half first: each key alone is a body, and `contentType` types
// the raw one. A refusal written one condition too wide fails here rather than
// at a review.
var $a = Core\Http\Client::post($url, {json: ["id" => 7]});
var $b = Core\Http\Client::post($url, {form: ["id" => "7"]});
var $c = Core\Http\Client::post($url, {body: "raw", contentType: "text/plain"});
var $d = Core\Http\Client::put($url, {multipart: ["part" => "value"]});

// And the refusal, which names the two keys that were *written* rather than
// the first two the bag declares.
var $e = Core\Http\Client::post($url, {form: ["id" => "7"], json: ["id" => 7]});
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0815]: `Core\Http\Client::post` is given two bodies, `form` and `json`
   --> case.nvs:19:61
%A
   = help: which key a body is written under is what says how it is sent, so exactly one of `json`, `form`, `body` and `multipart` may be written: keep the one whose encoding the server expects
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
